tests must be completed with MACE-matpes-r2scan-omat-ft.model. This file should be put in the data directory. It can be obtained from the mace-mp project

Also the files basis.xyz, control and potentials are required in this directory

Many of the tests (e.g. test_1) run on the GPU by default. The line 'device = "cuda"' can be changed to cpu if a GPU/cuda is not available.

test_1: tests whether some of the libraries are installed correctly - ASE, mace model, janus-core, numpy. It simply reads in basis.xyz using ASE and calculates an energy.

test_2: In addition to requirements for test1 a geometry optimisation is undertaken to check that this works.

test_3: Cetermines whether an single point energy calculated within pymc-nmr is the same as that from ASE
this checks some of the functionality on field, config, species modules

test_4:  Eetermines whether a relaxed energy calculated within pymc-nmr is the same as that from ASE
this checks some of the functionality on field, config, species modules

test_5: Determines energy of MC swap of a Si and Al atom to determine whther the value obtained is correct. The atoms are swapped back to check that the old energy is obtained (i.e. delta E is zero)

test_6: This test does the same as test_5 except that the configurations are optimised using the lbfgs routine from ASE

test_7: Determines energy of MC particle translation of an atom to determine whther the value obtained is correct. The atom is then moved back to check that the old energy is obtained (i.e. delta E is zero)

test_8: Tests MC displacement of volume (isotropic expansion) and calculates the energy. The volume is then returned to original state to check the original energy can be recalculated.

test_9: The same as test_8 except that anirotropic expansion is used instead.

test_10: The same test as test_1 except that the cpu is used rather than the GPU/cuda

test_11: The same as test_1 except that dispersion is used within the calculation

